1
00:00:00,218 --> 00:00:03,953
Transcatheter Aortic Valve
Implantation, or TAVI,

2
00:00:04,003 --> 00:00:08,575
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:08,898 --> 00:00:10,862
Heart in cut-away front view.

4
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:15,931
Blood leaves the left ventricle through
the aortic valve into the aorta.

5
00:00:16,338 --> 00:00:19,823
Severe aortic stenosis:
the calcified, stiff,

6
00:00:19,873 --> 00:00:22,954
tricuspid aortic valve
opens only partially,

7
00:00:23,004 --> 00:00:25,730
obstructing outflow from
the left ventricle.

8
00:00:26,071 --> 00:00:31,607
Severe aortic stenosis is defined by
valve area, gradient and velocity.

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:38,390
With symptoms or reduced LV function,
the Heart Team chooses TAVI or surgery.

10
00:00:39,229 --> 00:00:44,596
ECG-gated CT measures the annulus,
coronary heights and root anatomy,

11
00:00:44,646 --> 00:00:48,765
and assesses the iliofemoral
arteries for transfemoral access.

12
00:00:50,824 --> 00:00:55,205
Valve size is chosen from CT
annulus area or perimeter,

13
00:00:55,324 --> 00:00:57,336
using the manufacturer's chart.

14
00:00:57,929 --> 00:01:00,286
The values shown are illustrative.

15
00:01:00,804 --> 00:01:01,745
Step one.

16
00:01:02,326 --> 00:01:03,861
Transfemoral access:

17
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:08,111
a large-bore sheath is placed in
the femoral artery at the groin.

18
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:12,917
A catheter and guidewire are
advanced retrograde up the aorta,

19
00:01:12,967 --> 00:01:16,122
around the aortic arch,
to the ascending aorta.

20
00:01:16,538 --> 00:01:17,347
Step two.

21
00:01:17,976 --> 00:01:20,345
The guidewire crosses the narrowed valve.

22
00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:25,940
A stiff, pre-shaped wire is left
curled in the left ventricular apex.

23
00:01:26,371 --> 00:01:27,311
Step three.

24
00:01:27,928 --> 00:01:31,571
A temporary pacing wire is
placed in the right ventricle,

25
00:01:31,678 --> 00:01:34,071
via a vein, for rapid pacing.

26
00:01:34,424 --> 00:01:39,674
Optional: balloon valvuloplasty pre-dilates
the narrowed valve during rapid pacing.

27
00:01:40,004 --> 00:01:40,909
Step four.

28
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,585
The transcatheter valve, crimped
onto a balloon delivery catheter,

29
00:01:45,704 --> 00:01:48,454
is advanced over the wire around the arch.

30
00:01:48,858 --> 00:01:52,048
It is positioned across
the native aortic annulus,

31
00:01:52,108 --> 00:01:54,953
below the openings of
the coronary arteries.

32
00:01:55,338 --> 00:01:56,290
Step five.

33
00:01:56,847 --> 00:02:00,692
During rapid pacing, the balloon
expands the valve frame.

34
00:02:01,228 --> 00:02:06,169
The calcified native leaflets are
pushed aside against the aortic wall.

35
00:02:06,471 --> 00:02:07,423
Step six.

36
00:02:08,028 --> 00:02:09,778
The balloon is deflated.

37
00:02:10,385 --> 00:02:14,457
The delivery system, wires and
pacing wire are withdrawn,

38
00:02:14,528 --> 00:02:17,195
and the femoral access site is closed.

39
00:02:17,624 --> 00:02:22,408
Valve function is restored: the
new valve opens fully and closes,

40
00:02:22,458 --> 00:02:26,219
improving blood flow from the
left ventricle into the aorta.

41
00:02:26,658 --> 00:02:31,179
Immediately after deployment,
paravalvular leak, valve gradient,

42
00:02:31,229 --> 00:02:34,800
coronary flow, rhythm and
the access site are checked.

43
00:02:37,118 --> 00:02:42,091
Complications include stroke, conduction
disturbance needing a pacemaker,

44
00:02:42,141 --> 00:02:47,177
vascular injury and, rarely, coronary
obstruction or annular rupture.

45
00:02:48,138 --> 00:02:49,373
Careful selection,

46
00:02:49,423 --> 00:02:54,411
CT planning and post-deployment assessment
underpin a safe TAVI procedure.

47
00:02:57,158 --> 00:03:02,336
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

